Students who test poorly but demonstrate ability through portfolios, projects, and teacher assessments often achieve excellent outcomes in college — test-optional admissions at 1,800+ US universities has confirmed this.
Bilingual students systematically underperform on English-language tests even when their actual reasoning ability is equivalent — language proficiency confounds the measurement of intelligence.
The 'testing gap' between demographic groups narrows substantially when test prep access is equalised — confirming that the gap reflects access, not ability.
